<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Former Yan</h1><p class="st-meta">337 CE – 370 CE · capital Longcheng, Ye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Murong Xianbei kingdom that rose in Liaoning and briefly ruled the North China Plain from Ye, until the Former Qin conquered it in 370.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong Xianbei</a> settled in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> in the 3rd century and adopted farming and Chinese administration under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-hui">Murong Hui</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang">Murong Huang</a> declared himself King of Yan in 337, moving the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaoyang-liaoning">Longcheng</a> (Chaoyang) in 342. He destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yuwen-xianbei">Yuwen Xianbei</a> in 344 and sacked the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> capital in 342.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Former Yan combined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> cavalry with Chinese officials and farmers resettled from the war-torn plain. After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a> collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> conquered the north-east plain, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a> in 352, proclaimed himself emperor and moved the capital to Ye in 357. The regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> was a capable administrator and general.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> died, court jealousy drove out the talented general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, who had defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fangtou">Fangtou</a> in 369; he fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a>. In 370 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-meng-former-qin">Wang Meng</a> took Ye and captured the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> princes later founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-yan">Later Yan</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-yan">Western Yan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-yan">Southern Yan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/former-yan.jpg" alt="Former Yan" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_338_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>337 CE – 370 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Longcheng, Ye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Murong Huang, Murong Jun, Murong Wei</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, steppe shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yan (Murong)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=354&amp;focus=former-yan" aria-label="Former Yan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7547 750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Former Yan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7867 1006L7769 1191M7867 1006L7879 1125M7867 1006L7669 1147"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7879 1125h0M7669 1147h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7867 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7867 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=354&amp;focus=former-yan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

